abstract: >-
  webgpu-q is a research-grade quantum chemistry and many-body physics
  engine that runs entirely in a web browser via the WebGPU compute
  API. Methods include RHF/UHF/DFT/MP2/CCSD/UCCSD/CCSD(T), Cholesky
  density fitting (DF-HF, DF-MP2), and EE/IP/EA-EOM-CCSD for excited
  states / ionization / electron attachment. CCSD(T) on H2O cc-pVDZ
  is accelerated 13.8× (median of 20 trials after 5 warmup; 28.4× best,
  10.1× worst, std/median 42% — noisy) via a WGSL compute kernel,
  measured against this project's own single-threaded CPU TypeScript.
  The EOM-CCSD σ-equations are ports of PySCF's eom_gccsd matvecs,
  validated against brute-force projection of H̄ = e^(-T̂) H e^(T̂) in
  the full Fock space: EE and IP agree element-wise to < 1e-10 Ha and
  EA to < 5e-13 Ha on multi-electron LiH (T̂² ≠ 0). Many-body sector
  covers GPU statevector simulation (F ≥ 0.999999 vs CPU), MPS / DMRG
  with Lanczos and MPO (N = 128 TFIM/Heisenberg in browser), and kernel
  fusion (4.22× headline, Tier C 8×8 cascade). A browser-tab swarm partitions the
  density-fitted Fock build by auxiliary index across N same-origin
  tabs (BroadcastChannel + SharedArrayBuffer), reproducing the
  single-tab build to ~1e-15 and scaling Hartree-Fock to C60
  (300 basis functions, STO-3G) across four tabs on a 16 GB laptop.
